> If the behavior enabled by that constant in array_filter does not work when using the polyfill on older versions, it is better to not polyfill the constant so that code can detect the availability of the relevant feature by checking whether the constant is defined (instead of having to detect whether array_filter supports that constant).

Right, this is true. If the feature doesn't work, we really shouldn't polyfill it.

In this case, however, with the constant declared, you can call `array_filter($a, $b, ARRAY_FILTER_USE_VALUE)`, and it _will_ work across all PHP versions.

The only change in PHP 8.6 is that the default value `$mode = 0` is now set to the new constant (bearing `0`) `ARRAY_FILTER_USE_VALUE`.  But like I said in the other reply, it is the default value, which makes this a bit less useful.
